I rearranged the code and it seems to work.
WH_ <- function(., id){

  seize(.,"Shuttle")%>%
    seize(.,"Driver_Shuttle")%>%
    log_("going")%>%
    timeout(10)%>% #time spent to come to the warehouse
    delayed_release(env,"Driver_Shuttle",10)%>%

    seize(., paste0("FLT_UL_", id), 1) %>%
    timeout(50) %>%
    release(.,paste0("FLT_UL_", id), 1)%>%

    seize("Driver_Shuttle")%>%
    delayed_release(env,"Shuttle",10)%>%
    delayed_release(env,"Driver_Shuttle",10)%>%

    separate(.)%>% #is the equivalent of opening the shuttle
    log_("in the WH")
}

packstück<- trajectory("packstück transport") %>%
  batch(10, timeout=15, permanent=F)%>%

  #Shuttles go to 3 warehouses
  branch(function() sample(1:3, 1,prob = c(0.43,0.33,0.24)),continue=c(T,T,T),
         trajectory()%>%
           WH_(1),
         trajectory()%>%
           WH_(2),
         trajectory()%>%
           WH_(3)
  )
​
But now I have another question. I used the delayed release 2 times, for the wayback of the driver alone and the wayback of the shuttle. Since there is a separate() after the second delayed release, for some reason I have an end_time for the package taking into account also the delayed_release() time. Curiously, that doesn't happen with the batch time in the first delayed release. Why does that happen?

