Sorry, I didn't get this.

The problem is that get_mon_arrivals(per_resource=TRUE) doesn't have a column with the finished status. The way I managed was to get one table filtered by the other. Since I'm interested in the time spent from start (generation) to the end of the unloading process the code looks like this:

x<-get_mon_arrivals(env,per_resource=TRUE)%>%
  tidyr::spread(resource,start_time)
y<-filter(get_mon_arrivals(env),finished==T)

left_join(x,y,by="name")%>%
  group_by(name)%>%
  filter(finished==T)%>%
  summarise(total_time=max(Driver_Shuttle,na.rm=T)-max(start_time,na.rm=T))
​
Now with this time I can validate the simulation better, because it includes the waiting times but doesn't consider the effect of the clone.

Looks much better now, thanks a lot!!

